## File 4: src/middleware/errorHandler.js

```javascript
/**
 * @fileoverview Global error-handling middleware for Express.
 * 
 * DESIGN DECISIONS:
 * - This middleware must have 4 parameters (err, req, res, next) to be recognized by Express as error middleware.
 * - We handle both known AppError instances and unknown errors differently.
 * - In production, you would also log errors to a service like Sentry or Winston.
 */

const { AppError } = require('../errors');

/**
 * Global error-handling middleware.
 * 
 * This function is called automatically by Express whenever an error occurs
 * in any route handler or middleware. It ensures consistent error responses.
 * 
 * @param {Error} err - The error object that was thrown
 * @param {Object} req - Express request object
 * @param {Object} res - Express response object
 * @param {Function} next - Express next middleware function (not used here)
 */
function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  // Log the error for debugging (in production, use a proper logging service)
  console.error(`[ERROR] ${err.message}`);
  console.error(err.stack); // Print full stack trace

  // Determine if this is our custom AppError or an unknown error
  if (err instanceof AppError) {
    // Known application error - return the status code from the error object
    res.status(err.statusCode).json({
      success: false,
      message: err.message,
      statusCode: err.statusCode
    });
  } else {
    // Unknown server error - return 500 Internal Server Error
    console.error('Unknown error occurred:', err);

    res.status(500).json({
      success: false,
      message: 'Internal server error',
      statusCode: 500
    });
  }
}

module.exports = errorHandler;
```

**Why this pattern?** Express middleware with 4 parameters is automatically recognized as error-handling middleware. By placing this at the end of our middleware stack, it catches all unhandled errors from previous middleware and route handlers. This prevents crashes and ensures consistent JSON error responses.

