## Summary of Architectural Patterns Used

| Pattern | Where Applied | Why Chosen |
|---------|--------------|------------|
| **RESTful routing** | `routes/books.js` | Standard HTTP methods map naturally to CRUD operations |
| **Async/Await + asyncHandler** | All route handlers | Prevents unhandled promise rejections; clean error flow |
| **Custom Error Classes** | `middleware/errors.js` | Distinguish error types for appropriate HTTP status codes |
| **Global Error Middleware** | `server.js` (last middleware) | Centralized error formatting; never duplicate error-handling code |
| **In-Memory Data Store** | `data/books.js` | Zero setup for educational example; easy to swap for a database later |
| **Query Parameter Filtering** | `GET /books` | Flexible client-side filtering without additional endpoints |
| **Consistent Response Format** | All routes | `{ success, data/error }` structure makes the API predictable |
| **JSDoc Documentation** | Every function | Self-documenting code; works with IDE autocomplete and docs generators |
| **Modular File Structure** | Separate files per concern | Testable, maintainable, and scalable as the project grows |

This implementation provides a solid foundation for understanding REST API design in Node.js/Express. Each component can be extended independently — swap the data store for MongoDB, add authentication middleware, or implement pagination on the list endpoint — while keeping the overall architecture intact.

