## File 3: `middleware/errors.js`

```javascript
/**
 * @fileoverview Custom error classes and global error-handling middleware.
 *
 * WHY CUSTOM ERROR CLASSES? Built-in Error subclasses let us distinguish between
 * different failure modes (e.g., "not found" vs. "bad input") so route handlers
 * can respond with appropriate HTTP status codes. This is cleaner than checking
 * error messages as strings.
 *
 * WHY A GLOBAL ERROR MIDDLEWARE? Express supports a special 4-argument middleware
 * function `(err, req, res, next) => ...` that catches all errors — including
 * those thrown synchronously and rejected promises. Placing it last in the
 * middleware chain ensures it handles every error path uniformly.
 */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Custom Error Classes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/**
 * Base error class for all custom API errors.
 *
 * WHY EXTEND `Error`? It provides a standard stack trace and message property.
 * We add a `statusCode` property so the global handler can set the HTTP response
 * code without hardcoding it in each route.
 */
class ApiError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Human-readable error description.
   * @param {number} statusCode - HTTP status code (default: 500).
   */
  constructor(message, statusCode = 500) {
    super(message); // Call the parent Error constructor
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    // Capture the stack trace for debugging
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

/**
 * Error thrown when a requested resource is not found.
 * Maps to HTTP 404 Not Found.
 */
class NotFoundError extends ApiError {
  /**
   * @param {string} resourceName - Name of the missing resource (e.g., "Book").
   * @param {*} id - The ID that was requested.
   */
  constructor(resourceName = "Resource", id) {
    super(`${resourceName} with id ${id} not found`, 404);
  }
}

/**
 * Error thrown when client input fails validation.
 * Maps to HTTP 400 Bad Request.
 */
class ValidationError extends ApiError {
  /**
   * @param {string|string[]} message - Validation error message(s).
   */
  constructor(message) {
    super(typeof message === "string" ? message : message.join("; "), 400);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Global Error-Handling Middleware
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/**
 * Express middleware that catches all errors and formats a consistent JSON response.
 *
 * WHY PLACE THIS LAST? Express executes middleware in order. By placing the error
 * handler at the end of the chain, we ensure it runs after all route handlers
 * and other middleware have had a chance to process the request. Any error that
 * bubbles up (via `next(err)`) will be caught here.
 *
 * @param {Error} err - The error object thrown by a route handler or middleware.
 * @param {import('express').Request} req - Express request object.
 * @param {import('express').Response} res - Express response object.
 * @param {import('express').NextFunction} next - Express next middleware function.
 */
function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  // Log the full error details to the console for debugging
  console.error(`[Error] ${err.statusCode || 500}: ${err.message}`);
  console.error(err.stack);

  // Build a consistent JSON error response
  /** @type {{status: number, message: string, code?: string}} */
  const errorResponse = {
    status: err.statusCode || 500,
    message: err.message,
  };

  // Optionally include an error code for programmatic clients
  if (err instanceof NotFoundError) {
    errorResponse.code = "NOT_FOUND";
  } else if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    errorResponse.code = "VALIDATION_ERROR";
  }

  // Send the JSON response with the appropriate status code
  res.status(errorResponse.status).json(errorResponse);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module Exports
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

module.exports = {
  ApiError,
  NotFoundError,
  ValidationError,
  errorHandler,
};
```

**Why this error architecture?**

- **Custom classes** (`NotFoundError`, `ValidationError`) let route handlers throw typed errors that the global middleware can inspect to set correct HTTP status codes.
- **Global handler** as a 4-argument middleware is Express's standard pattern for centralized error handling. It prevents duplicate error-handling code in every route.
- **Consistent JSON shape** (`{status, message, code}`) makes it easy for API consumers to parse errors uniformly.

